for one-shot normal commands from insert or replace mode
.El
.Pp
Counts are supported across a large subset of motions and operator-pending
commands.
A count on
.Cm i ,
.Cm a ,
.Cm I ,
.Cm A ,
.Cm o ,
.Cm O
or
.Cm R
enters the text that many times;
.Cm r
replaces that many characters, or none when the line has fewer;
.Cm s
changes that many characters, as many as the line has;
.Cm cc
and
.Cm S
change that many lines, and fail, changing nothing, when fewer than
count \- 1 lines follow the current one; with
.Ic autoindent
set, the new line takes the first changed line's indent.
A count on
.Cm \&.
repeats the change's text that many times.
The
.Cm \&.
command repeats the last
.Cm d ,
.Cm <
or
.Cm >
with its motion, whatever the motion, a search's pattern included; a count
on
.Cm \&.
replaces the operator's.
.Pp
For
.Cm w ,
.Cm b
and
.Cm e
a word is a run of letters, digits and underscores, or a run of other
non-blank characters; for
.Cm W ,
.Cm B
and
.Cm E ,
a run of non-blanks.
An empty line is a word.
The word motions cross lines, also after an operator, except that the
last word a
.Cm w
moves over after an operator ends at the end of its line;
.Cm cw
on a non-blank changes to the end of the word, and on a blank changes
that blank alone.
.Cm %
on a bracket moves to its match; elsewhere, to the match of the first
bracket after the cursor on the line.
A
.Cm d
over more than one line that starts in its first line's indent and leaves
only blanks after it deletes the whole lines.
The current implementation still has documented parity gaps in a few motion,
undo, and repeat corner cases; see
.Pa docs/specs/exvi.md
for the tracked conformance backlog.
.Pp
A search pattern, as an ex command's, is a POSIX basic regular expression
.Pq Xr regex 3 ,
with
.Ql \e<
and
.Ql \e>
matching at the start and end of a word, and
.Ql ~
standing for the replacement text of the last
.Ic :substitute .
With the
.Ic magic
option off,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&[ ,
.Ql *
and
.Ql ~
are ordinary characters and their escaped forms are the special ones.
The replacement of
.Ic :substitute
is read as
.Xr ex 1
describes:
.Ql & ,
.Ql \e1
to
.Ql \e9 ,
.Ql ~
and the case escapes.
.Pp
A control character is drawn as
.Ql ^ Ns Ar X ,
and a byte that is no printable character \(em a byte in no valid UTF-8
sequence, or one of a C1 control's \(em as
.Ql \e Ns Ar ooo ,
its octal value: no byte of a file reaches the terminal as one it would
act on.
.Pp
Text input,
.Ql r
and
.Ql R
take any character typed, UTF-8 included;
.Ql r
and
.Ql R
replace the whole character under the cursor, and in
.Ql R
backspace restores it.
.Ql Ctrl-@
inserts the text last entered and ends text input;
.Ql Ctrl-A
inserts it and goes on.
.Ql Ctrl-H ,
.Ql Ctrl-W
and
.Ql Ctrl-U
erase only what the text input entered on the current line \(em one
character; blanks and then a word, a run of letters, digits and
underscores or of other characters; all of it \(em and leave the buffers
alone;
.Ql u
undoes the input, erasures and all, as one change.
An
.Aq ESC
followed within 50 milliseconds by keys that make no terminal key sequence
(an arrow key is
.Aq ESC
.Ql \&[C )
is an
.Aq ESC
and then those keys, so typing fast or pasting loses nothing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl R
Start in readonly mode.
Plain writes are rejected unless forced with
.Ql \&! .
.It Fl r
Recover the named file from a preserved recovery image.
.It Fl s
Suppress interactive diagnostics where possible, and read no startup
commands from
.Ev EXINIT ,
.Pa ~/.exrc
or
.Pa ./.exrc .
Commands given with
.Fl c
still run.
.It Fl S
Start in secure mode.
Shell escapes and shell-capable commands are disabled.
.It Fl v
Accepted for shared parser compatibility.
When invoking
.Nm
directly it keeps visual mode active.
.It Fl c Ar command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
This option may be specified more than once.
.It Fl t Ar tag
Start by editing the file and location named by
.Ar tag .
.It Ar +command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
Common forms include
.Ql +42
to move to line 42 and
.Ql +next
to advance within the argument list.
.It Ar file
Open
.Ar file
as the current buffer.
If multiple files are given, they become the initial argument list.
.El
.Sh SET OPTIONS
.Ic :set
takes every option POSIX lists (see
.Xr ex 1 ,
which lists them); these are the ones with an effect in
.Nm :
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ic number , nu
Display line numbers in the visual gutter.
.It Ic list , li
Display tabs and line endings in list form.
.It Ic autoindent , ai
Reuse current-line indentation for
.Cm o ,
.Cm O ,
split-newline, and linewise change/substitute helpers.
.It Ic ignorecase , ic
Make search, repeat-search, and word-search commands case-insensitive.
.It Ic readonly , ro
Mark the session readonly.
.It Ic showmode , smd
Show or suppress insert and replace mode banners.
.It Ic wrapscan , ws
Wrap searches at buffer boundaries.
.It Ic magic
Give
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&[ ,
.Ql *
and
.Ql ~
their special meaning in patterns.
It is on by default.
.It Ic writeany , wa
Let
.Ic :write
replace an existing file other than the current one without
.Ql \&! .
It is off by default.
.It Ic exrc , ex
Read
.Pa ./.exrc
at startup.
It is off by default and has effect only when set by
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc .
.It Ic tabstop= , ts=
Set tab rendering width.
The default is 8.
.It Ic shiftwidth= , sw=
The step of
.Cm >> ,
.Cm << ,
.Cm Ctrl-T
and
.Cm Ctrl-D :
.Cm >>
and
.Cm <<
shift a line's indent by it,
.Cm Ctrl-T
and
.Cm Ctrl-D
in text input to the next or previous multiple of it.
.Cm Ctrl-D
changes the indent only when nothing but the indent comes before the
cursor;
.Ql 0
.Cm Ctrl-D
typed after the indent removes it, and
.Ql ^
.Cm Ctrl-D
removes it for the current line only.
The default is 8.
.It Ic scroll= , sc=
Set the half-page scroll amount after it is explicitly configured.
.It Ic tags=
Set the comma-separated tag-file search path used by
.Ql Ctrl-]
and
.Ic :tag .
.It Ic directory= , dir=
The directory under which the user's recovery directory,
.Pa exvi- Ns Ar uid ,
is kept.
The default is
.Pa /var/tmp .
.It Ic shell= , sh=
The program that runs the commands of
.Ic :! ,
.Ic :r \&!
and
.Ic :w \&! ,
as
.Ar shell Fl c Ar command .
The default is
.Ev SHELL ,
or
.Pa /bin/sh
when that is unset.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev EXINIT
If set, supplies startup commands, and
.Pa ~/.exrc
is not loaded.
Ignored with
.Fl s .
.It Ev SHELL
The default of the
.Ic shell
option.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa ~/.exrc
Per-user startup commands, loaded when
.Ev EXINIT
is not set, and only when ownership and directory safety checks pass.
.It Pa ./.exrc
Per-directory startup commands, loaded after
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc
only if one of them set the
.Ic exrc
option, only when the ownership and directory safety checks pass, and not
when it is
.Pa ~/.exrc
itself.
Neither
.Pa .exrc
is loaded with
.Fl s
or
.Fl S .
.It Pa tags
Default tag file name when the
.Ic tags
option is left at its default value.
Each line is a tag name, a file name and an address, separated by tabs.
The address is a line number or a
.Li / Ns Ar pattern Ns Li /
or
.Li \&? Ns Ar pattern Ns Li \&?
search, and nothing else: any other address is reported as a bad tag
address, never run as a command.
.It Pa /var/tmp/exvi- Ns Ar uid
The user's recovery directory, under the
.Ic directory
option's value: preserved recovery images, one per file, written by
.Ic :preserve
and for sessions ended by
.Dv SIGHUP
or
.Dv SIGTERM .
It is created with mode 0700 and used only while it is the user's, not a
symbolic link and closed to everyone else; each image is created afresh
with mode 0600, and only an image the user owns is recovered.
Writing the file removes an image only if this session wrote or recovered
from it.
.El
.Sh ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS
In visual mode
.Ql Ctrl-C
is an ordinary key.
A
.Dv SIGINT
sent to
.Nm
acts as
.Aq ESC :
it ends text input, and cancels a partial command or a
.Ql \&:
or search prompt.
.Dv SIGHUP
and
.Dv SIGTERM
preserve a modified buffer and end the session, restoring the terminal.
.Pp
.Ql Ctrl-Z
in command mode restores the terminal and stops
.Nm
with
.Dv SIGTSTP ;
on
.Dv SIGCONT
it takes the terminal back and redraws the screen.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success and with a value greater than 0 if an error occurs.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vi file.txt
vi +42 file.txt
vi -R file.txt
vi -t main tags-demo.c
vi -c 'set number' file.txt
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The supported Substrate contract is intentionally narrower than the union of
historical BSD, nvi, and Vim behavior.
Only the command names, abbreviations, motions, options, and entry points
documented here and in
.Pa docs/specs/exvi.md
are part of the supported interface.
.Pp
Readonly and restricted entry points such as
.Cm view
and
.Cm rvi
are implemented through the shared frontend parser even though they are not
separate binaries today.
.Pp
The current renderer still uses a repaint-heavy refresh strategy rather than a
dirty-region diff engine.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ex 1 ,
.Xr regex 3
